### SORU 27/50 ###
Explain how a rainbow forms, using only the analogy of sunlight passing through a prism.
######################################################################

Sure! Imagine you have a magical prism that can split white light into its beautiful colors just like a rainbow does in nature. When sunlight enters this prism, it encounters the glass surface at an angle.

Now, think of each color as having slightly different "weight" or speed when traveling through air versus glass. The red light is relatively heavy (or slow) compared to other colors, while violet light is quite light (or fast). As the sunlight hits the prism, these differences cause the various colors to bend or refract by different amounts—a process called dispersion.

The light then bounces off the inside surface of the prism before exiting back into the air. By the time it exits, all those differently colored lights have been bent enough so they spread out from one another, creating a spectrum of colors—red on one side and violet on the other. This sequence of colors looks exactly like what we see in a rainbow!

So, just like a rainbow appears after raindrops act as tiny prisms in the sky, our magical prism splits white light into its component colors due to their varying speeds and angles of refraction. And there you have it—an explanation of how a rainbow forms, but now visualized through the lens of a prism!

